#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158425} :activity/id #uuid "6b889e54-831f-5d09-99ae-ffd6954a561f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049096 :topic/id #uuid "da2dfe4a-bc54-5e1d-95c2-c38fd31bbb1b" :topic/math-academy-id 2798} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158578 :tutorial/content "In day-to-day life, we use informal language that is linguistically more expressive but may lack the rigor needed in formal mathematics.\n\nFor instance, consider the following example of a sentence in informal language:\n\n*The weather is fine.*\n\nNotice that this statement is a bit vague:\n\n- The weather is fine where? It could be fine in some places and not so much in others.\n- What is meant by \"fine\"? Some people like sunny weather, while others prefer less hot, cloudy days.\n\nIn contrast, formal language, as seen in mathematical proofs, follows strict syntax (rules for constructing sentences) and semantics (rules for interpreting sentences).\n\nIn formal language, statements are sometimes expressed symbolically using the logical quantifiers $\\forall$ and $\\exists.$ For example:\n\n$\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ $n^2 \\geq 0.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will practice translating from plain English to formal mathematical expressions.\n\nFor example, let's express the statement below in formal language:\n\n*Every real number is either positive or negative*.\n\nLet's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"every\" indicates we should use a universal quantifier, $\\forall.$\n- The sentence refers to real numbers. So, by denoting a real number as $x,$ we have that $$x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$$\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as $$x > 0 \\: \\textrm{ or } \\: x < 0.$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$$\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x > 0 \\lor \\: x < 0$$\n\n**Note:** We can use $\\lor$ (disjunction) instead of \"or\". Also, this statement is false (since $0$ is also a real number), though this does not affect the translation process." :tutorial/id #uuid "afbca8d0-d9c9-5cd6-bdbd-8ed2e2be8373" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8000 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "019d456c-ec74-52c8-bfa4-6ad58515c9bc" :step/math-academy-id 69040 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157009 :question/id #uuid "9a2cc32a-e163-5259-a419-fd1da1072cb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12753"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c5ab572-f91e-503a-9ece-7b3c890d41c4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050878}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390219}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Plain English Sentence as a Formal Statement"} :step/id #uuid "a4ec1df1-b49f-53ef-8bca-2365ec833cf0" :step/math-academy-id 69041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157008 :question/id #uuid "e01fcb05-3eef-5fa0-9657-fa186c058d09" :question/math-academy-id "e-12752"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4a372ba-1e69-54c2-a8f1-50011e4561ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414821}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Plain English Sentence With Two Quantifiers as a Formal Statement"} :step/id #uuid "386bcbd6-bbe6-5fb4-b92a-179d189e5b4c" :step/math-academy-id 69044 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157007 :question/id #uuid "6bf3ba6b-3476-5450-b0b9-24623630f56d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12751"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc2780ae-9b3a-5b4e-8bb6-54182f2f861b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414820}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Implicit Quantification"} :step/id #uuid "076043e4-6afb-5ba6-827d-2b2323a1eba7" :step/math-academy-id 69047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158583 :tutorial/content "If a sentence contains a variable but does not have any quantifiers, it's *implicitly* implied that there must be a universal quantifier:\n\n$$P(x) \\: \\equiv \\: \\forall x \\, P(x)$$\n\nwhere the domain of $x$ is usually clear from the context.\n\nFor example, given that $x$ is an integer, the sentence\n\n*If $6 \\mid x,$ then $3 \\mid x$*\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, (6 \\mid x) \\Rightarrow (3 \\mid x).$$\n\nLet's now consider an example of an *implicit* existential quantifier.\n\n*The square root of $5$ is a real number.*\n\nNotice that this sentence does not contain any variables. However, it can be re-phased as follows:\n\n*There exists a real number $x$ such that $\\sqrt{5}=x.$*\n\nWe can also write this as:\n\n*There exists a real number $x$ such that $x^2=5.$*\n\nIn formal language, we write this as follows:\n\n$$\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x^2=5$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eb4e81dd-6130-575b-bde4-9a1d8990f1d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8338 :tutorial/title "Implicit Quantification"} :step/id #uuid "6fec7fae-516f-51aa-994b-d0c3278296e2" :step/math-academy-id 69051 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158428}}] :activity/title "Formal and Informal Language" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157466}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414822} :activity/id #uuid "24aca76c-cd7b-52c5-8c39-35bfa198e405" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049096 :topic/id #uuid "da2dfe4a-bc54-5e1d-95c2-c38fd31bbb1b" :topic/math-academy-id 2798} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e031a5-7a9d-5b98-a333-297f4dcf2049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,{n}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414790} {:answer/id #uuid "32105362-0ced-5e88-8fe5-ab722dec7a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,{n}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414791} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1c18c4-be8d-5184-94b8-464bbe35189d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,{n}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414794} {:answer/id #uuid "4f49044b-e864-553d-97f7-caf6b86e5d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,n\\in Z,\\,n={25}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414797} {:answer/id #uuid "ed83f2a1-4943-51dd-a25a-58cd8ef533dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,n={25}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414812}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414790} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2ee9a6-68f1-5dfd-96f1-bb4d9adfa772" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "491ff3c4-7e3c-503f-8510-b201ed62a106" :question/math-academy-id "q-172161" :question/problem "$25$ is a perfect square.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Recall that \"$25$ is a perfect square\" means the following:\n\nThere exists an integer $n$ such that ${n}^{2}=25.$\n\nMore formally, this reads as follows:\n\n$∃\\,n\\in Z,\\,{n}^{2}=25$"} :step/id #uuid "5cc4daae-92a4-562a-b0bc-69db33a25a95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21397aad-294d-5cb0-8fc2-55534f4a6fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,n\\in N,\\,∀\\,x\\in R,\\,x+n>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414789} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec06de9-0d99-57b0-9a3a-16fc00cab7e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,x+n>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414796} {:answer/id #uuid "75fd620c-3826-539d-aadf-0db05cc1c187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,x+n>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414801} {:answer/id #uuid "884a72c8-4262-5b6c-97e1-72f59632519a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,xn>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414806} {:answer/id #uuid "9effbd25-3f4e-52ec-adfe-e05327867b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,n\\in N,\\,∃\\,x\\in R,\\,x+n>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9937ecd-67a0-55f8-9d21-e9472a02b1f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "61ea11f5-e114-5b4e-93aa-fef7b636de63" :question/math-academy-id "q-183569" :question/problem "There is a real number such that, added to any natural number, gives a positive number.\n\nExpress the above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our sentence can be re-phrased as \n\nThere exists a real number $x$ such that, added to any natural number, gives a positive number.\n\nNow, \"$x$ added to any natural number gives a positive number\" means that\n\nFor any natural number $n,$ we have that $x+n>0.$\n\nTherefore, we get the following statement:\n\nThere exists a real number $x$ such that for any natural number $n$ we have that $x+n>0.$\n\nMore formally, this reads as\n\n$∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,x+n>0.$"} :step/id #uuid "684e8d56-c781-5037-9fa3-b779df0822ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36385d37-b616-5435-802e-28750f745704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,{k}^{2}=512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414793} {:answer/id #uuid "43a6f437-2d46-5135-bb5c-65973d31af6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,k\\in Z,\\,{2}^{k}=512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414795} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba09872-e3ca-54c9-806a-1e8cf5a8c8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,{2}^{k}=512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414800} {:answer/id #uuid "927875d6-c6c6-5ddb-a6ea-953be6ea498a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,k\\in Z,\\,k=\\sqrt{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414807} {:answer/id #uuid "d95df757-4f9e-5360-a232-168c68f04785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,k={512}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414800} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5da8752-1f0d-59e0-a94c-e374bf67e5d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f1922dc-ffa7-525a-929e-f25f8887b492" :question/math-academy-id "q-183556" :question/problem "$512$ is a power of base $2.$\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Recall that \"$512$ is a power of base $2$\" means the following:\n\nThere exists an integer $k$ such that ${2}^{k}=512.$\n\nMore formally, this reads as follows:\n\n$∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,{2}^{k}=512$"} :step/id #uuid "7d4c67a0-d61f-5e10-ac71-f09f134d7a7a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14428b06-3047-50b9-aa88-3ad9212e487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,k\\in Z,\\,∃\\,q\\in Q,\\,kq=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414788} {:answer/id #uuid "337eee28-d0ff-5774-974d-6f7b31464bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,∃\\,q\\in Q,\\,kq=q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414792} {:answer/id #uuid "7bad8008-4b08-5444-bc02-a16600d66a30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,q\\in Q,\\,∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,kq=q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414802} {:answer/id #uuid "84a0f7dc-748f-5977-98f4-cc7c7bcb7a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,∀\\,q\\in Q,\\,kq=q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414803} {:answer/id #uuid "c314ce38-5463-5799-a600-f517694af5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,q\\in Q,\\,∀\\,k\\in Z,\\,kq=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414803} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd95aa99-f4e5-52d5-a54e-5119eca0a5a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f4dd7341-6f18-5e73-afaa-e4cf5a0832e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-183715" :question/problem "There exists an integer number such that, for any rational number, the product of both numbers is the second number.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The words \"there exists\" indicate that we use an existential quantifier, $∃.$\n\n- The word \"any\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The sentence refers to integers and rational numbers. So, by denoting our numbers as $k$ and $q,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,∀\\,q\\in Q.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nkq=q\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,∀\\,q\\in Q,\\,kq=q$"} :step/id #uuid "d0fd9634-715c-5f74-8009-1d3e9fd3b761" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60f48153-5303-511e-a5c1-204d5bb84306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,{x}^{k}\\in Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414798} {:answer/id #uuid "665dd058-0658-5bbd-a0ab-dcb1fc441209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,{x}^{k}\\in Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414799} {:answer/id #uuid "857fcb90-58e7-5169-a4e8-cdffb26d856a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,k\\in Z,\\,∀\\,x\\in R,\\,{x}^{k}\\in Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414804} {:answer/id #uuid "87bd3680-2669-50c3-a706-c4f21ca5a8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∀\\,k\\in Z,\\,∃\\,x\\in R,\\,{x}^{k}\\in Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414805} {:answer/id #uuid "cfdf3270-1913-5243-b0a3-4b9066c9a5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,k\\in Z,\\,{x}^{k}\\in Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414810} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cdc6710-ca1a-5981-b0a2-0186ffd3f509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "822441e0-0d3a-577b-9cb1-929e6c39db8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-183717" :question/problem "There is a real number such that, raised to any integer, the result is a rational number.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The words \"there exists\" indicate that we use an existential quantifier, $∃.$\n\n- The word \"any\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The sentence refers to real numbers and integers. So, by denoting our numbers as $x$ and $k,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,k\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{k}\\in Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,k\\in Z,\\,{x}^{k}\\in Q$"} :step/id #uuid "dfab1f2b-4555-5bd9-9f40-20d004a7cae8"}] :activity/title "Formal and Informal Language" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414787}]}
